1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Let's look at how you add waves.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,600
Adding waves is called superposition.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:11,099
Now if I had two crests hitting towards each other in a spring,

4
00:00:11,099 --> 00:00:19,100
then when the two crests meet, they create a wave or something for a fraction of a second as they're passing through each other.

5
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:22,199
And they would create something that's bigger than both of them.

6
00:00:22,199 --> 00:00:26,600
If this crest was five centimeters and this crest was four centimeters,

7
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:31,000
then when they meet, they would create a crest with an amplitude of nine centimeters.

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,500
We call this a super crest.

9
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:42,500
Very similarly, when two troughs meet, if this one was four centimeters and this one was five centimeters,

10
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:47,000
below the axis, then that would add up to nine centimeters below the axis.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,299
So I'm going to give what I call a super trough.

12
00:00:54,399 --> 00:00:58,000
The amazing thing about waves is when the two waves pass through each other,

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,199
they may create some new shape, but they actually pass right through each other unaffected.

14
00:01:03,199 --> 00:01:08,700
And the way you could tell that is, if I'm standing there talking to someone and they're talking to me and we're speaking at the same time,

15
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:11,900
their sound wave travels through my sound wave.

16
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:15,700
And when their sound wave gets to me, it sounds like them and I can hear what they're saying.

17
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:21,200
And my sound wave travels through their sound wave and when it gets to them, they can understand what I'm saying.

18
00:01:21,299 --> 00:01:27,299
If the two waves mix and then create a new wave that change the properties of each individual wave,

19
00:01:27,299 --> 00:01:31,100
then when two people are talking to each other, it would get all garbled up.

20
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,299
Also, radio waves do the same thing.

21
00:01:33,299 --> 00:01:37,800
Two radio waves can be passing by in here and I can tune in one radio wave or the other,

22
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:40,000
even though they're passing through each other.

23
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,100
When it comes to waves and adding them,

24
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:46,900
sometimes we get waves like this, like the square wave and the sort of triangular looking wave.

25
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,500
We want to add those two up.

26
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:54,300
We use the same idea of adding the amplitudes to find out what's going to happen.

27
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:59,900
So I can make a quick sketch and say there's the square wave and there's the triangular wave.

28
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:01,300
Let's see what happens.

29
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:02,600
So look at the beginning.

30
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,700
The square wave is five centimeters tall.

31
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:06,300
The triangular wave is zero.

32
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:08,400
So my first point would be five.

33
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:09,599
Then I go over here.

34
00:02:09,599 --> 00:02:12,700
I might have the rectangular wave.

35
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:15,400
The square wave is five and the triangle is one.

36
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:16,599
So that's six.

37
00:02:16,599 --> 00:02:21,200
If I work my way along the whole thing doing that, I'll end up with a wave like this.

38
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:25,200
So when these two waves meet, I might get a shape that looks like that.

39
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,000
It can be a lot more complicated when you have waves like this.

40
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,800
In order to find out what the result is when this wave and this wave are meeting each other,

41
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,000
I have to do the same thing.

42
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,700
I add up the amplitudes, but here it's so much busier.

43
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:40,500
I have to do one piece at a time.

44
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:43,400
I have to start over here and say zero plus zero is zero.

45
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:45,300
This wave is three high.

46
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:47,000
This wave is one below.

47
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,700
So three up, one down is two.

48
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:50,500
So I put a dot at two.

49
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:53,500
And you literally have to work your way all the way through this.

50
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:58,000
Going one on one space at a time and figuring out what it's going to be.

51
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,300
In the end, you'll end up with some weird wave that might look a little odd,

52
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,500
but that's what's going to happen.

53
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:07,200
That looks a little bit like what you see in a sound wave.

54
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:11,300
When you look on a oscilloscope, you see this very complex looking wave.

55
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:16,600
That complex looking wave is actually made up of all the simple waves put together.

56
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:22,200
And computers are really good at taking this complex wave and distilling it down to the simple waves.

57
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:25,700
We can find out what waves went into making that up.
